D2 = [
# ---------------- AI PRODUCT GROWTH ----------------
("AIPG","Hard","Claude has 50M MAU. How would you get to 100M in 6 months without increasing the research team?",
 "Growth model decomposition.","Split into acquisition, activation and retention; use distribution and product levers, not research.",
 "Doubling in six months needs about 12% monthly growth. I would model sources: new users (organic, referral, partnerships), reactivation, and reduced churn. Levers that need no research headcount: partnerships and integrations (browsers, phones, productivity suites, education), shareable outputs and artifacts that bring people in, templates for the top ten jobs to improve activation, student and team programmes, localisation for large markets, and lifecycle messaging for dormant users. Prioritise by size and speed, run experiments weekly, and watch quality and cost so growth does not degrade the product. I would give a range and name the two bets that carry most of the upside.",
 "Listing tactics without a growth model.","12% monthly growth; partnerships, shareable outputs, templates, localisation, reactivation.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","Your AI code assistant has 5M developers. GitHub Copilot has 50M. How do you compete with 1/10th the budget?",
 "Competitive strategy under constraints.","Pick a wedge where you can win; avoid fighting on their strengths.",
 "I would not compete on breadth or distribution. Choose a wedge: a segment with unmet needs (for example large legacy codebases, a specific language or framework, security-sensitive teams, or agentic multi-file workflows), best quality and trust in that wedge, and word-of-mouth through developer communities and open-source maintainers. Offer superior context handling, transparent pricing, and integration with tools they already use. Grow through bottom-up adoption with a team plan, then enterprise. Measure retention, weekly active developers in the wedge, NPS and expansion within teams.",
 "Trying to match features and spend.","Pick a wedge, win trust and quality there, grow bottom-up, then expand.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","ChatGPT has 800M MAU but a 20% DAU/MAU ratio. How would you double daily usage in one quarter?",
 "Habit and frequency.","Understand why most users are occasional; create daily use cases.",
 "A 20% ratio means most users come a few times a month. Doubling is a big ask, so I would find high-frequency jobs (writing, coding, study help, planning, quick answers) and build daily triggers: morning briefings, tasks and reminders, memory that makes the product more personal, integrations into calendars and email, widgets and voice shortcuts, and notifications when long tasks finish. Target segments with natural daily need (students, professionals). Measure DAU/MAU by cohort and use case, sessions per user and retention. Honestly, the goal may be a smaller uplift in one quarter; I would set a target range and sequence experiments.",
 "Spamming notifications.","Find daily jobs, add routines, memory and integrations; measure DAU/MAU by use case.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","You're launching an AI image generator in a market dominated by Midjourney. What's your GTM strategy?",
 "Go-to-market against an entrenched leader.","Pick a segment, a differentiator and a channel.",
 "Choose a differentiated position, not a generic 'better images'. Options: ease for non-designers (inside tools people already use), commercial-safe licensed training for brands, consistency across characters and campaigns, or speed and price. Target a specific segment such as marketers or e-commerce sellers. Go-to-market: integrate into tools they use (slides, design apps, store builders), offer templates for their jobs, partner with agencies, and use showcase communities. Free credits to try, team plans to convert. Measure activation (first usable image), weekly use, paid conversion and share of outputs used in published work.",
 "Competing on quality claims alone.","Segment plus differentiator plus channel; integrations, templates, trial credits.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","Your AI writing assistant has 100K users. Grammarly has 30M. How do you get to 1M users in 6 months?",
 "Scale-up from small base against an incumbent.","Find a growth loop and a segment the incumbent serves poorly.",
 "10x in six months needs a loop, not just ads. I would target a niche where generic grammar help is not enough (academic writing, sales emails, fiction, non-native English professionals) and build a shareable output or collaboration loop (documents shared with reviewers, citations, templates). Add distribution through browser extensions, integrations with email and docs, creators and communities, and campus programmes. Optimise onboarding to reach a first win in two minutes. Track activation, week-4 retention, referral rate and cost per activated user. If retention is weak, fix it before scaling acquisition.",
 "Buying growth with poor retention.","Niche plus loop; extensions, integrations, creators; fix retention first.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","Perplexity has grown to 10M MAU in 2 years. You're building an AI search competitor. What's your growth strategy?",
 "Entering a competitive category.","Differentiate by job, vertical or trust; use distribution wedges.",
 "General search is dominated by incumbents. I would pick a vertical or job where AI answers beat links: research, shopping, travel, finance or health information with trusted sources. Differentiators: transparent sourcing, depth, freshness, and workflow (save, export, collaborate). Growth: browser default and extension, shareable answer pages that rank in search, partnerships with publishers and creators, and communities of professionals. Measure answer satisfaction, return rate, queries per user and citation click-through. Monitor cost per query because search at scale is expensive.",
 "A 'better answers' pitch with no wedge.","Vertical wedge, trusted sources, shareable answer pages, extension, watch cost per query.") ,
("AIPG","Medium","Your AI productivity tool has a 2% viral coefficient. How would you get to 1.0+ in 3 months?",
 "Understanding virality.","Viral coefficient = invites per user x conversion. Improve both and the product's shareable moments.",
 "A coefficient of 1.0 means each user brings one new user, which is very hard. I would map the loop: when does a user share, how many people see it, how many convert? Increase invites per user by building collaboration (shared workspaces, shared outputs), and increase conversion by making the invited experience excellent (landing page with context, no signup friction). Add rewards where value-aligned. Realistically I would aim for a meaningful increase (say from 0.02 to 0.2) and combine virality with paid and partnership channels, and I would say honestly that 1.0 is rarely reached. Track invites sent, accepted, activated and time to second use.",
 "Promising 1.0; adding generic referral bonuses without a natural shared moment.","Map the loop, add collaboration and shareable output, optimise invitee conversion, set realistic target.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","ChatGPT Plus has 10M subscribers at $20/month. You're building Claude Pro. How do you get to 5M subscribers?",
 "Subscription growth strategy.","Define target users, value gap, conversion funnel, and constraints.",
 "Reaching half of a leader's paid base needs a clear reason to pay. I would identify segments with strong willingness to pay (developers, writers, analysts, students) and give them value that is hard to match: long context, strong writing and coding, projects with persistent knowledge, and reliable limits. Improve free-to-paid conversion with usage limits at value moments, trials, annual and team plans, and workplace purchases. Expand distribution through partnerships and integrations. Estimate the funnel: free users x conversion rate; 5M paid at 5% conversion requires 100M monthly free users, so growth and conversion both matter. Track conversion, churn and revenue per user.",
 "Ignoring the free-user base needed for the target.","Segments with willingness to pay, differentiated value, conversion funnel math.") ,
("AIPG","Medium","Your free AI product has great retention but 99% never convert to paid. How do you increase paid conversion?",
 "Monetisation of retained users.","Find why free is enough; create a paid tier worth paying for.",
 "High retention means value exists; the paid tier does not add enough or is gated badly. I would identify power users and what they do, interview users who hit limits, and design premium value for them: higher limits, advanced models or features, collaboration, history and exports, priority speed, and integrations. Place the upgrade at value moments rather than blocking early use. Test pricing, trials and annual plans. Watch free-user experience, since over-gating hurts retention. Target segments such as professionals and teams first. Metrics: conversion by segment, revenue per user, churn and impact on retention.",
 "Paywalling core value early.","Segment power users, premium features, upgrade at value moments, test pricing.") ,
("AIPG","Medium","An AI health app has 1M downloads, but 80% never complete onboarding. You have 6 weeks to fix activation.",
 "Onboarding funnel diagnosis.","Measure each step; reduce friction; deliver value earlier.",
 "Week 1: instrument the funnel step by step and watch sessions to see where people stop (permissions, long forms, sensitive questions, account creation). Week 2 to 4: shorten and reorder: show value first (a quick insight or plan), ask only what is needed, explain why sensitive data is requested, allow skip and later completion, and add progress indicators and trust signals (privacy, credentials). Test variants. Week 5 to 6: roll out winners and add reminders for incomplete onboarding. Success: completion from 20% to 40%, plus quality of activation (first meaningful action). For health, keep safety and privacy high.",
 "Cutting questions that are needed for safe advice.","Funnel diagnosis, value before asks, trust signals, experiments, reminders.") ,
("AIPG","Medium","Your AI meeting assistant has 500K users but only 50K use it weekly. How would you increase weekly active usage?",
 "Frequency and habit.","Make usage automatic and outcomes visible.",
 "Weekly use falls when users must remember to start the tool. I would automate: connect to calendars and join meetings by default, deliver recaps and action items automatically to email and chat, and send weekly digests across meetings. Add team features so colleagues rely on it, search across past meetings, and manager views. Learn from the 10% who are active: what workflow do they use? Build onboarding around that. Metrics: share of meetings recorded, recap opens, actions created, weekly active ratio.",
 "More features rather than automation.","Auto-join, auto-deliver recaps, team features; copy what active users do.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","You're launching an AI tutoring app against Khan Academy and Duolingo. What's your differentiation strategy?",
 "Positioning against strong incumbents.","Choose a specific learner and outcome; use AI where it creates a clear advantage.",
 "Incumbents have content, brand and habit loops. Differentiate with personalised one-to-one tutoring that adapts to a learner's gaps, explains in their own words, handles any question, and supports exam goals (for example board exams, entrance tests, job interviews) in local languages. Trust matters: accuracy checks, teacher and parent views, and safety for minors. Start with one subject and market, prove learning outcomes, then expand. Metrics: learning gain, weekly active learners, retention and referrals from parents and teachers.",
 "Promising to replace teachers; ignoring accuracy and safety.","Specific learner and exam goal, adaptive tutoring, local language, outcomes proof.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","Your AI design tool has PMF with designers but wants to expand to marketers. How do you grow into this segment?",
 "Segment expansion.","Understand marketer jobs; adapt product, messaging and channels.",
 "Marketers need speed, brand consistency, templates and collaboration, not pixel control. Research their top jobs (social posts, ads, landing pages, campaigns). Build templates, brand kits, one-click resizing, approvals, scheduling integrations and analytics links. Change messaging to outcomes (more content in less time). Channels: marketing communities, agencies, integrations with ad and email tools, and partnerships. Beware of confusing the core designer experience. Run a pilot with 20 teams, measure activation (first campaign asset published), team seats and retention.",
 "Marketing the designer product unchanged.","Learn marketer jobs, add templates and brand kits, outcome messaging, pilot.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","ChatGPT's growth plateaued at 800M MAU. You need to find 200M new users. Where do they come from?",
 "Market sizing and growth vectors.","Identify under-penetrated segments and barriers.",
 "Think by barrier: awareness, access, language, usefulness and trust. Candidates: non-English and emerging markets (local languages, lighter apps, cheaper plans, voice), older adults and non-tech workers (simple use cases), students and schools, workplaces where tools are blocked (enterprise offerings), and devices and operating system integrations. For each, estimate the size and conversion barrier, and pick two or three bets. Measure reach, activation and retention per segment, and watch quality and cost of serving at scale.",
 "Assuming the same users will use it more instead of finding new groups.","Segments by barrier: language, access, age, enterprise, devices; size and prioritise.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","Your AI customer support tool has 10% of the market. Zendesk is adding AI features. How do you defend and grow?",
 "Defending against incumbents.","Differentiate on outcomes and depth; lock in via integration and data.",
 "Incumbents bundle AI. Defend by being clearly better at resolution quality, not feature count: measurable automation rates, accuracy with citations, handoff quality, multilingual support and fast setup. Deepen integrations with order, billing and CRM systems so the AI can act, not just answer. Provide analytics that tie support to revenue and retention. Pricing aligned to outcomes (per resolution). Grow with case studies and vertical templates. Build switching cost through data, workflows and trust, not lock-in tactics. Watch churn and win rates versus the incumbent.",
 "Competing only on price.","Outcome quality, actions via integrations, analytics, outcome pricing.") ,
("AIPG","Hard","You're building an enterprise AI product. How do you grow from 10 customers to 100 in one year?",
 "Enterprise sales and product-led motion.","Repeatable ICP, proof, pipeline and delivery capacity.",
 "Define the ideal customer profile from the best of the 10: industry, size, use case and buyer. Package a repeatable offer with a clear ROI story and a 30-day pilot. Build proof: case studies, references, benchmark results. Channels: outbound to the ICP, partners and system integrators, marketplaces, and community. Ensure onboarding and support can scale (templates, security documentation, self-serve admin). Track pipeline, pilot to contract conversion, time to value, expansion and churn. Adding ten customers a month needs a funnel of qualified opportunities; plan capacity so quality does not collapse.",
 "Custom work for every customer.","ICP, repeatable pilot and ROI proof, partners, scalable onboarding.") ,

# ---------------- AI PRODUCT LAUNCH ----------------
("AIPL","Hard","OpenAI wants to build an AI scheduling assistant. You have 8 months and a team of 10. What do you build?",
 "0-to-1 product scoping.","Define user and job, MVP, phases, risks and success metrics.",
 "Start with a user: busy professionals coordinating meetings across calendars and time zones. Jobs: find a time, handle conflicts, protect focus time, and reschedule. MVP (months 1 to 3): connect calendars, propose times by email or chat, respect preferences, and ask before acting. Months 4 to 6: negotiation with external guests, travel and buffer awareness, team availability. Months 7 to 8: scale, admin controls, analytics. Principles: user approval for changes, transparent reasoning, privacy of calendar data. Metrics: scheduling success without back-and-forth, time saved, weekly active users, trust (undo rate). Risks: errors causing missed meetings, privacy, integration breadth.",
 "Building everything at once; no approval controls.","Focused MVP with approval, grow to negotiation, track success and undo rate.") ,
("AIPL","Hard","Anthropic wants to compete with Cursor for AI code editors. What's your product strategy?",
 "Competitive product strategy.","Find the wedge, advantages and sequencing.",
 "Competitors win on editor experience and speed. A strategic angle: lean on model strengths in long context and reasoning for large repositories, agentic multi-file changes with strong review and safety, and deep integration with existing editors and terminals instead of forcing a new editor. Start with developers who value reliability on big codebases. Features: repo understanding, test-driven edits, clear diffs, permissions, team policies. Distribution: bottom-up developer adoption, enterprise security. Metrics: accepted changes, task completion, weekly active developers, retention and team expansion.",
 "Copying the editor feature by feature.","Wedge on big codebases and agentic reliability; integrate rather than replace; measure accepted changes.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","You're launching an AI meeting assistant in a market with Otter, Fireflies, and Grain. How do you differentiate?",
 "Crowded-market positioning.","Choose a segment, a unique outcome and a channel.",
 "Transcription is a commodity. Differentiate on outcomes and a segment: for example sales teams (CRM updates and coaching), product teams (decisions and tasks into planning tools), or healthcare and legal (compliance). Add privacy and compliance features, accuracy with domain vocabulary, and automation of follow-ups. Channel: integrations and marketplaces plus vertical communities. Measure activation (first useful recap), recurring use, and workflow completions (CRM updated, tasks created).",
 "Pitching better transcription.","Pick a segment and outcome, integrate follow-up workflows, add compliance.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","Google wants to add AI features to Gmail. You have 6 months for v1. What do you ship?",
 "Feature prioritisation for a huge-scale product.","High-frequency jobs, low risk, high trust, rollout safety.",
 "Pick jobs people do daily: summarise long threads, draft and polish replies, extract actions and dates, search in natural language, and triage. v1 should be assistive, not autonomous: drafts that users edit and send, with clear controls. Roll out gradually to opted-in users, with privacy promises (no training on personal emails without consent), enterprise admin controls and an easy off switch. Evals: tone, accuracy of summaries, safety. Metrics: adoption, edit rate of drafts, time saved, satisfaction and complaints.",
 "Auto-sending emails; ignoring privacy.","Thread summaries, drafts, search, actions; opt-in, privacy and admin controls.") ,
("AIPL","Hard","Design an AI-powered search product for e-commerce that could challenge Google Shopping.",
 "Product concept with marketplace dynamics.","Use AI where search fails: intent, comparison and trust. Address supply and distribution.",
 "Shoppers struggle with vague intent, comparison and trust. An AI shopping search could understand natural requests ('a gift for a runner under 3,000'), ask clarifying questions, compare products with sources, combine reviews and price history, and explain trade-offs. Supply: partner with retailers via feeds and affiliate links, keep prices and stock fresh. Trust: transparent ranking, labelled ads, authentic reviews. Challenges: product data quality, sponsored placement, distribution. Start with a category (electronics) and a trusted brand angle. Metrics: successful purchase sessions, return rate, revenue per session, and trust ratings.",
 "Ignoring data supply and monetisation conflicts.","Intent understanding, comparison, trust; start in one category; partner for data.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","You're building an AI tutoring product for high school students. What's your MVP and launch strategy?",
 "MVP definition in education.","One subject, one goal, proof of learning, safety.",
 "MVP: one subject (for example maths) and one exam goal. Core: diagnostic test to find gaps, step-by-step guided practice rather than just answers, explanations in plain language, and progress tracking. Safety: age-appropriate content, no personal data collection beyond need, parental visibility. Launch: pilot with a few schools or tutoring centres, collect learning gains, then open to students with parent involvement. Metrics: improvement on pre/post tests, sessions per week, retention, and parent satisfaction. Pricing later, after proving outcomes.",
 "Giving answers without teaching.","One subject, diagnostic, guided practice, safety, pilot with outcome measurement.") ,
("AIPL","Hard","Microsoft wants to build an AI project management tool using GPT-4. What would you build in 12 months?",
 "Phased roadmap.","Integrate into existing workflows; AI for planning, tracking and reporting.",
 "Quarter 1: AI for creating plans from goals, breaking work into tasks, and drafting status updates from activity. Quarter 2: risk and dependency detection, workload balancing and estimates based on history. Quarter 3: natural-language queries across projects, auto-generated reports for executives, meeting-to-task flows. Quarter 4: automation and approvals, enterprise governance, and extensibility. Principles: humans confirm changes, transparency of sources, permissions. Metrics: time saved on planning and reporting, accuracy of estimates, weekly active teams, expansion. Risks: poor data quality and trust in estimates.",
 "Autonomous scheduling without oversight.","Plan generation, status drafts, risk detection, query and reports; governance.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","Design an AI customer support product for SMBs. How is it different from enterprise solutions?",
 "Segment-specific design.","SMBs need speed, low cost and simplicity.",
 "SMBs have small teams, limited technical skills and tight budgets. The product should set up in an hour: connect website, help docs and email, answer common questions, hand off to humans with context, and work across chat and WhatsApp. Pricing is simple and predictable. Enterprises need complex integrations, compliance, analytics and customisation. For SMBs, provide templates by industry, pre-built answers, and a dashboard showing resolved questions and time saved. Metrics: setup time, automation rate, CSAT, retention.",
 "Building enterprise features for SMBs.","Fast setup, templates, simple pricing, human handoff.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","You're launching an AI creative writing tool. Sudowrite and Jasper exist. What's your angle?",
 "Differentiation in a crowded space.","Pick a specific writer and workflow.",
 "Choose a segment (novelists, screenwriters, marketers, students) and a workflow that tools handle poorly: for example long-form consistency (characters, world and style memory), outline-to-draft with revision tracking, or collaboration with editors. Offer control (style sliders, rewriting suggestions) and keep the writer's voice. Address trust: ownership of content and no training on user text. Channels: writer communities and creators. Metrics: words written with the tool, project completion, retention.",
 "A general 'write anything' pitch.","Specific writer, long-form memory, voice control, ownership promise.") ,
("AIPL","Hard","Stripe wants to build an AI fraud detection. You have 4 months for beta. What's the scope?",
 "Scoping an ML product with high stakes.","Start narrow; measure cost of errors; include human review.",
 "Scope the beta to one fraud type and a subset of merchants with good data (for example card-not-present fraud). Build a scoring model using transaction, device and behaviour signals with explainable reason codes; integrate with existing rules and let merchants choose actions (allow, review, block). Include a feedback loop of disputes and chargebacks, monitoring dashboards and a rollback plan. Run in shadow mode first, then limited live use. Metrics: fraud caught, false positives, approval rate, review workload, latency. Define thresholds from the cost of fraud versus lost sales.",
 "Blocking transactions with no review or explainability.","One fraud type, shadow mode, reason codes, merchant controls, cost-based thresholds.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","Build an AI-powered job search platform. How is it different from LinkedIn?",
 "Differentiation against a network.","LinkedIn has network and supply; compete on job-seeker experience and outcomes.",
 "Do not replicate the network. Focus on the seeker's workflow: understand skills and goals, find matched roles from many sources, tailor applications, prepare for interviews, and track progress. Trust matters: show why a role matches, avoid spammy listings, and protect privacy. Sell to seekers first with a free core and paid coaching, then employers who want quality candidates. Metrics: interviews per applicant, time to offer, retention, and employer response rates.",
 "Trying to build a professional network.","Seeker workflow, matching with explanations, tailored applications, outcomes.") ,
("AIPL","Medium","You're designing an AI personal finance advisor for Gen Z. What's your product thesis?",
 "Thesis and product principles.","Define the user insight and the trust approach.",
 "Gen Z often lacks financial guidance and distrusts institutions. The thesis: a friendly coach that turns messy money into simple next steps (budget, save, invest basics, debt) using their data with consent. Features: spend insights, goals, automated small savings, and clear explanations. Guardrails: no personalised investment advice without regulation, transparent fees, security, and clear limits. Monetise through partnerships or subscription, avoiding conflicts of interest. Metrics: savings rate, active goals, retention and financial confidence.",
 "Giving regulated advice without compliance.","Coach for next steps, insights and automation, trust and compliance.") ,
("AIPL","Hard","Create an AI legal document assistant for small law firms. What's your go-to-market?",
 "Regulated vertical GTM.","Trust, accuracy and workflow fit decide adoption.",
 "Small firms value saved time but fear errors. Product: drafting and review of common documents (contracts, NDAs), clause comparison with sources, and redline suggestions, all with lawyer review. Go-to-market: start with a niche practice area, partner with bar associations, offer a pilot with measurable time saved, and build credibility via expert advisors and accuracy reports. Pricing per seat with usage limits. Provide confidentiality guarantees, audit logs and no training on client data. Metrics: hours saved per matter, adoption per lawyer, error rate found in review, renewal.",
 "Marketing it as replacing lawyers.","Niche practice area, lawyer-in-the-loop, confidentiality, measurable time saved.") ,
("AIPL","Hard","Design an AI medical diagnosis assistant for primary care doctors. What are your constraints?",
 "High-stakes AI product thinking.","Constraints: safety, regulation, liability, workflow, evidence.",
 "Constraints: patient safety (errors can harm), regulatory approval as a medical device in many regions, data privacy (health data laws), clinical validation with real evidence, explainability, liability and the doctor's final responsibility, integration with records and workflow, bias across populations, and clinician trust. Design as decision support: suggest possible diagnoses and next tests with evidence and uncertainty, never final answers. Start with narrow conditions where evidence is strong. Evaluate with clinicians, prospective studies and monitoring. Metrics: diagnostic accuracy support, time saved, clinician satisfaction and safety events.",
 "Presenting AI as the decision maker.","Decision support, narrow scope, evidence, regulation, privacy, bias, clinician in control.") ,
]
